    content: `
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-4 font-display">What Are LH and FSH?</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Luteinizing Hormone (LH) and Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) are gonadotrophins — messenger hormones produced by the pituitary gland at the base of the brain. Together, they orchestrate the menstrual cycle, ovarian follicle development, ovulation, and sex hormone production.</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-4 text-slate-600 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>FSH (Follicle-Stimulating Hormone):</strong> Stimulates the growth and maturation of ovarian follicles (the fluid-filled sacs that contain developing eggs). Rising FSH levels in the early part of the menstrual cycle signal follicles to grow and produce oestrogen.</li>
        <li><strong>LH (Luteinizing Hormone):</strong> Triggers ovulation — the release of a mature egg — when it surges mid-cycle. After ovulation, LH stimulates the resulting corpus luteum to produce progesterone, which supports the uterine lining in the second half of the cycle (luteal phase).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Both hormones are regulated by the hypothalamus (through GnRH — Gonadotrophin-Releasing Hormone) in a pulsatile fashion. The brain, pituitary, and ovaries constantly communicate through this feedback system to maintain cycle regularity.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">What Is the LH/FSH Ratio?</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The LH/FSH ratio is simply the value of LH divided by the value of FSH, measured from a blood test at a specific point in the cycle. It emerged historically as a marker because in many women with PCOS, LH levels tend to be proportionally higher than FSH levels during the early follicular phase.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">What Is a Normal LH to FSH Ratio?</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">In an ovulatory menstrual cycle, when LH and FSH are measured during the <strong>early follicular phase (Day 2, 3, or 4 of menstruation)</strong>, both hormones are present in relatively similar concentrations. The physiological LH/FSH ratio during this window is typically close to <strong>1:1</strong> — meaning LH and FSH are approximately equal.</p>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">However, this ratio changes significantly throughout the cycle:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-4 text-slate-600 space-y-2">
        <li>At mid-cycle ovulation, LH surges dramatically — so testing at this point would show a very high LH/FSH ratio, which is entirely normal and expected.</li>
        <li>Ratios measured on different cycle days or under different circumstances are not comparable and cannot be meaningfully interpreted without knowing the cycle timing.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mb-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-blue-800 font-semibold"><i class="fas fa-info-circle mr-2"></i>The timing of your LH and FSH blood test critically determines what the numbers mean. A test done on Day 14 of your cycle (at ovulation) will look very different from one done on Day 2–3 (early follicular), and the "normal" interpretation changes accordingly.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">What Does an Elevated LH/FSH Ratio Mean?</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">When the LH/FSH ratio is measured correctly — in the <strong>early follicular phase</strong> — a ratio elevated above approximately 2:1 or 3:1 is sometimes considered a supportive feature of PCOS.</p>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">In some women with PCOS, a higher-than-usual hypothalamic GnRH pulse frequency preferentially promotes pituitary LH secretion over FSH. This elevated LH then over-stimulates the ovarian theca cells to produce androgens (testosterone), while the relatively lower FSH means no follicle is selected to grow to full maturity and ovulate. The result is irregular or absent ovulation.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">Does an LH/FSH Ratio Greater Than 2 Diagnose PCOS?</h2>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mb-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-amber-800 font-semibold"><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation mr-2"></i><strong>Important:</strong> The 2023 International Evidence-based Guideline for the Assessment and Management of PCOS explicitly states that the LH/FSH ratio is <em>not</em> a reliable or recommended standalone diagnostic criterion for PCOS. It has been removed from the current diagnostic algorithm used by international specialist bodies.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">There are several reasons why the ratio alone is insufficient:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-4 text-slate-600 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Pulsatile secretion:</strong> LH is released in bursts (pulses) from the pituitary, meaning a single blood draw catches the LH level at one arbitrary moment. It could be caught at a peak or a trough of a pulse, making a single LH measurement highly variable.</li>
        <li><strong>Not present in all PCOS cases:</strong> Many women who have PCOS — confirmed by the current Rotterdam diagnostic criteria — do not have an elevated LH/FSH ratio. The ratio has limited sensitivity as a diagnostic tool.</li>
        <li><strong>Other conditions can elevate the ratio:</strong> Stress, recent weight changes, and other hormonal conditions can also affect LH levels. A ratio alone cannot distinguish between causes.</li>
        <li><strong>PCOS diagnosis is clinical:</strong> Current guidelines require at least two of three features — irregular/infrequent periods, clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound or elevated AMH. The LH/FSH ratio is not part of this framework.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">When LH Is Higher Than FSH</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">If LH is notably higher than FSH during the early follicular phase (and it is not due to being close to ovulation), possible considerations include:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-4 text-slate-600 space-y-2">
        <li>PCOS (as a supportive — not diagnostic — feature)</li>
        <li>Normal mid-cycle ovulatory LH surge (if the test was done around Day 12–16)</li>
        <li>Stress-related fluctuations in hypothalamic signaling</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">A gynaecologist or endocrinologist will interpret elevated LH in context — alongside other hormones, your menstrual history, ultrasound findings, and AMH levels — rather than basing decisions on the ratio alone.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">When FSH Is Higher Than LH</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">When FSH significantly exceeds LH during the early follicular phase, this is a different clinical signal and is generally not associated with PCOS. It may suggest:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-4 text-slate-600 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Diminished ovarian reserve:</strong> As the ovaries have fewer follicles remaining, the pituitary works harder and secretes more FSH to recruit follicles. Elevated early-follicular FSH (particularly when above 10–12 mIU/mL, though laboratory thresholds vary) can indicate reduced ovarian reserve and is relevant for fertility planning.</li>
        <li><strong>Perimenopause or premature ovarian insufficiency (POI):</strong> Very high FSH alongside low oestrogen is characteristic of the menopausal transition or, when it occurs before age 40, premature ovarian insufficiency (also called premature menopause).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">FSH higher than LH is a different scenario from "high LH/FSH ratio" and requires its own clinical evaluation.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">When Should LH and FSH Be Tested?</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">To ensure clinical accuracy, serum LH and FSH should be collected under standardised conditions:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-4 text-slate-600 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Cycle day:</strong> Early follicular phase — <strong>Day 2, 3, or 4</strong> of spontaneous menstruation (Day 1 = first day of full menstrual flow)</li>
        <li><strong>Time of day:</strong> Morning (8–10 am) is preferred due to diurnal hormone variation</li>
        <li><strong>For women with very irregular or absent periods:</strong> A doctor may advise a progestin-withdrawal bleed to artificially create a Day 1, after which Day 2–3 testing can occur. Alternatively, random testing with additional context may be used.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">What Other Tests Are Typically Considered Alongside LH/FSH?</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">LH and FSH alone rarely provide a complete clinical picture. A doctor investigating menstrual irregularity, PCOS, or fertility concerns will often also consider:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mb-4 text-slate-600 space-y-2">
        <li>Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) — now considered a more reliable marker of ovarian reserve and follicle activity than FSH</li>
        <li>Oestradiol (E2) — collected alongside LH/FSH on Day 2–3</li>
        <li>Total and free testosterone / DHEA-S (androgens)</li>
        <li>Prolactin and TSH (to exclude other causes of irregular cycles)</li>
        <li>Pelvic ultrasound (ovarian morphology and antral follicle count)</li>
        <li>Fasting insulin and glucose (HOMA-IR if metabolic factors are suspected)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4 font-display">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-5 mt-4">
        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">What is a normal LH to FSH ratio on Day 3?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">During the early follicular phase (Day 2–4), both LH and FSH are typically in the range of 2–8 mIU/mL in a normally ovulating woman, yielding a ratio close to 1:1. Ratios around 1:1 are generally considered physiologically normal at this time in the cycle. However, exact reference values differ between laboratories and assay methods, and clinical interpretation always requires full context.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">My LH/FSH ratio is greater than 2. Does that mean I have PCOS?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Not necessarily. An LH/FSH ratio above 2:1 on a correctly timed Day 2–3 sample may be a supportive observation in PCOS, but it is not a diagnostic criterion and is not present in all PCOS cases. Current international guidelines (2023 PCOS Guideline) do not include the LH/FSH ratio in the diagnostic algorithm. A full assessment by a gynaecologist or endocrinologist — including your period history, androgen levels, and ultrasound findings — is needed for any diagnosis.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Can the LH/FSH ratio change month to month?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. Due to the pulsatile nature of LH secretion, even two blood tests on consecutive days can yield different LH values. Stress, acute illness, significant weight changes, and the exact moment in the LH secretory pulse at which the sample is drawn all affect the result. This variability is one key reason why the ratio is not considered a reliable standalone diagnostic tool.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">What does a high LH mean if I have irregular periods?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Elevated LH in the context of irregular periods warrants further investigation. The most common cause in reproductive-aged women is PCOS. However, other causes — including thyroid dysfunction, hyperprolactinemia, and, in older reproductive-aged women, early perimenopause — should also be assessed. A doctor will correlate the LH result with FSH, oestradiol, AMH, prolactin, TSH, and androgen levels.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">My FSH is higher than my LH. What does this mean?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">When FSH significantly exceeds LH during the early follicular phase, this pattern is unrelated to PCOS. It may suggest diminished ovarian reserve, perimenopause, or — if you are under 40 — premature ovarian insufficiency (POI). The absolute FSH value, alongside AMH, oestradiol, and antral follicle count on ultrasound, provides much more useful information than the ratio alone. Please discuss these results with a gynaecologist or reproductive endocrinologist.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Does the LH/FSH ratio matter for fertility?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">LH and FSH levels individually — rather than just their ratio — are more useful for fertility assessment. FSH and AMH are commonly used to estimate ovarian reserve (egg quantity and quality). LH is monitored in fertility treatment cycles to time procedures. The ratio alone has limited utility in the modern fertility evaluation framework, which has largely moved toward AMH as the primary ovarian reserve marker.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Can stress cause an abnormal LH/FSH ratio?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. Chronic psychological or physical stress can dysregulate hypothalamic GnRH pulsatility, which in turn alters LH and FSH secretion patterns. This is another reason why a single LH/FSH ratio result should not be interpreted in isolation or used to make clinical decisions without a broader assessment of your overall health and hormonal picture.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Should I see a gynaecologist or endocrinologist about my LH/FSH results?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">If your LH/FSH results are outside the expected range for the correct phase of your cycle, or if you have irregular periods, difficulty conceiving, or symptoms of androgen excess, consulting either a gynaecologist or reproductive endocrinologist is appropriate. They can interpret the results in the context of your full clinical picture and order any additional investigations needed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 p-5 bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This article is written for educational purposes and reviewed by HealNari's clinical advisory panel. It does not constitute medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Hormone test results must always be interpreted by a qualified healthcare professional in the context of your individual clinical history and the laboratory reference ranges provided with your specific test report.
      </div>
    `
